@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: World-Systems Theory and Dependency Theory
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 16 total (High: 6, Medium: 10, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, citation chaining (S2)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
World-systems theory (Wallerstein) and dependency theory (Frank, Amin, and
their Latin American and African interlocutors) extend historical
materialism's core categories — mode of production, class, exploitation —
from the nation-state or single social formation to the capitalist
world-economy as a whole. Wallerstein's "modern world-system" treats the
world-economy inaugurated in the "long sixteenth century" as the true unit
of analysis, structured into core, periphery, and semi-periphery zones
linked by an international division of labor. Frank's "development of
underdevelopment" thesis holds that peripheral "satellite" regions are not
pre-capitalist survivals awaiting modernization but products of the same
world-capitalist process that produces metropolitan development — the two
are dialectically bound. Amin generalizes this into a theory of "unequal
exchange" and "accumulation on a world scale," in which value is
systematically transferred from periphery to core through international
trade at prices that do not reflect underlying labor values, entrenching
peripheral "extraversion" and blocked autocentric development.

This current provoked one of the sharpest intra-Marxist methodological
disputes of the late twentieth century. Robert Brenner's 1977 essay (here
verified via its 1982 anthology reprint) charged Wallerstein and Frank with
"neo-Smithian Marxism": by locating the origin and reproduction of
capitalism in exchange relations and the world market rather than in
historically specific relations of production and class struggle, they
allegedly collapse into the same circulationist logic as Adam Smith,
substituting a generic "commercialization" story for Marx's analysis of
capitalist social-property relations. This charge (elaborated further by
Political Marxism, covered in a separate domain of this review) forced
world-systems and dependency theorists to clarify whether "exploitation"
and "class" apply at the level of the world-system as a whole (a zone of
producers versus a zone of appropriators) or must remain anchored in
concrete relations of production within a bounded social formation — the
same fault line that runs through the transition-to-capitalism debates.
Weeks and Dore's Marxist critique of unequal-exchange theory anticipates
Brenner's charge from within the dependency tradition itself, while later
reassessments (Kvangraven, Song, Féliz) revisit the dispute, arguing that
Brenner's "trade versus production" dichotomy obscures the ways dependency
and world-systems theorists did theorize production relations, only at a
different (global) scale of analysis.

Recent scholarship (Chian, Geneciran, de Oliveira and Kvangraven, Pradella)
recovers the anti-colonial and non-Eurocentric stakes of this current,
tracing Wallerstein's turn to Marxism through his engagement with African
decolonization, situating dependency theory's 1972 Dakar conference
(organized by Amin) as a suppressed origin-point of a Global South
political economy, and reconnecting Marx's own writings on colonialism to
this world-scale framework. This positions world-systems/dependency theory
not merely as a sociological variant of Marxism but as the current that
most explicitly forces historical materialism to reckon with its own
Eurocentric starting assumptions.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the review's paradigm case of historical materialism's
application "beyond Europe" and beyond the single social formation, and it
supplies the clearest documented instance of an intra-Marxist
methodological schism (circulationism/"neo-Smithian Marxism" vs.
production-centered class analysis) that parallels and intersects with the
transition-to-capitalism debates treated in the Political Marxism domain.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Primary theoretical statements by Wallerstein (beyond the 2004 introductory
volume) and Frank's original 1966 "The Development of Underdevelopment"
essay were not independently recoverable with verified bibliographic
metadata through the available search tools within this session; secondary
literature discussing them was used instead. OpenAlex access was
intermittently rate-limited during this session, constraining the breadth
of that source relative to Semantic Scholar and citation chaining. Three
entries (wallerstein2004worldsystems, amin1976accumulation,
amin2010lawworldwide) are marked INCOMPLETE: no verified abstract could be
located for the two Amin books or the Wallerstein book through CORE,
Semantic Scholar, or OpenAlex; an NDPR match initially returned for Amin's
"The Law of Worldwide Value" was for an unrelated book (Postema's Law's
Rule) and was discarded rather than retained, so this entry is INCOMPLETE
rather than carrying a mismatched abstract.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Pair Brenner's neo-Smithian-Marxism charge directly against Kvangraven's,
Song's, and Féliz's reassessments to stage the live methodological dispute;
use Chian, Geneciran, and de Oliveira/Kvangraven to develop the
Eurocentrism/decolonial dimension as a distinct axis of evaluation from the
"trade vs. production" axis, since the two debates (methodological rigor
vs. geographic/epistemic scope) are often conflated in secondary
commentary.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- World-systems/dependency (circulationist, per Brenner's critics): 4 papers
  (Wallerstein 2004, Amin 1976, Amin 2010, Petras 1981) — capitalism and
  exploitation are properties of the world division of labor as a whole,
  mediated through unequal exchange.
- Political-Marxist/production-centered critique: 1 primary paper (Brenner
  1982) plus its direct reception (Denemark and Thomas 1988) — capitalism
  is a historically specific set of class/social-property relations, and
  "development of underdevelopment" mistakes market dependence for
  capitalist exploitation.
- Reassessment/synthesis positions: 4 papers (Kvangraven 2020, Song 2015,
  Féliz 2021, Weeks and Dore 1979) — arguing the trade/production dichotomy
  is overdrawn and that dependency theory has its own (underappreciated)
  production-centered variants.
- Decolonial/intellectual-historical recovery: 5 papers (Chian 2025,
  Geneciran 2024, de Oliveira and Kvangraven 2023, Pradella 2017, Stephens
  2016, Kay 2011) — situating the school's non-Eurocentric ambitions and
  internal intellectual development.
====================================================================
}

@book{wallerstein2004worldsystems,
  author = {Wallerstein, Immanuel},
  title = {World-Systems Analysis: An Introduction},
  publisher = {Duke University Press},
  year = {2004},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wallerstein presents the modern world-system — not the nation-state or individual society — as the proper unit of historical-social analysis, tracing the emergence of a capitalist world-economy in the "long" sixteenth century, structured into core, periphery, and semi-periphery zones bound together by an international division of labor and a single world market, and periodized through successive phases culminating in the crises of the twentieth century.

  RELEVANCE: This is the primary methodological statement against which Brenner's "neo-Smithian Marxism" charge and all subsequent defenses and revisions of world-systems analysis must be read; it establishes the scale-shift (world-economy rather than social formation) that this domain's entire debate with orthodox and Political Marxism turns on.

  POSITION: Foundational statement of world-systems analysis as an alternative unit of analysis to the nation-state-centered variants of historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {world-systems-theory, core-periphery, Wallerstein, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{brenner1982neosmithian,
  author = {Brenner, Robert},
  title = {The Origins of Capitalist Development: A Critique of Neo-Smithian Marxism},
  booktitle = {Introduction to the Sociology of "Developing Societies"},
  publisher = {Macmillan Education UK},
  year = {1982},
  pages = {54--71},
  doi = {10.1007/978-1-349-16847-7_4},
  abstract = {The appearance of systematic barriers to economic advance in the course of capitalist expansion—the "development of under-development"—has posed difficult problems for Marxist theory. There has arisen, in response, a strong tendency sharply to revise Marx's conceptions regarding economic development. In part, this has been a healthy reaction to the Marx of the Manifesto, who envisioned a more or less direct and inevitable process of capitalist expansion: undermining old modes of production, replacing them with capitalist social productive relations and, on this basis, setting off a process of capital accumulation and economic development more or less following the pattern of the original homelands of capitalism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Brenner argues that Wallerstein and Frank, in explaining capitalist development and underdevelopment through the world market's division into exploiting core and exploited periphery, effectively reproduce Adam Smith's commercialization model of economic development rather than Marx's analysis of historically specific class relations of production; he labels this "neo-Smithian Marxism" because it displaces class struggle over the relations of production from the center of the analysis in favor of a generic logic of trade and profit-maximization operating at the level of the world market.

  RELEVANCE: This is the direct textual source of the "neo-Smithian Marxism" charge this domain is tasked with tracing; it is the founding document of the dispute between world-systems/dependency theory and the production-centered, class-relational current (elaborated as Political Marxism in a separate domain of this review) over whether "capitalism" and "exploitation" can be theorized at the scale of the world-economy at all.

  POSITION: Class-relational critique of world-systems and dependency theory as a circulationist ("neo-Smithian") departure from Marx's theory of capitalist relations of production.
  },
  keywords = {neo-smithian-marxism, brenner-critique, world-systems-theory, dependency-theory, class-relations, High}
}

@article{denemarkthomas1988brennerwallerstein,
  author = {Denemark, Robert A. and Thomas, Kenneth P.},
  title = {The Brenner-Wallerstein Debate},
  journal = {International Studies Quarterly},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {32},
  number = {1},
  pages = {47--65},
  doi = {10.2307/2600412},
  abstract = {One of the most critical evaluations of Immanuel Wallerstein's world-systems perspective comes from Marxists who dislike the dominant role played by trade as opposed to class interaction in his analysis. At the forefront of this critique is Robert Brenner, whose article in New Left Review, "The Origins of Capitalist Development: A Critique of Neo-Smithian Marxism," has elicited far less of a debate than is warranted. In the first part of this article we carefully outline the various parts of this important critique, briefly drawing attention to some of the much more fundamental issues each addresses. In the second part we consider one of the most important of these issues, that of the most appropriate level of analysis for understanding political phenomena. The debate over this point revolves largely around events in Poland in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. Thus this rather arcane topic takes center stage in an argument with far broader implications. We conclude that while one should maintain a wider system level of analysis, more attention must be paid to the concrete determinants of power within political units as well.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Denemark and Thomas reconstruct the dispute between Brenner and Wallerstein as centering on two distinct, largely incommensurable questions — Brenner's concern with the causal origins of capitalism in specific agrarian class relations versus Wallerstein's concern with the systemic reproduction and expansion of an already-existing world-economy — and argue that much of the polemical heat between the two positions stems from talking past one another on the unit and level of analysis rather than a straightforward empirical disagreement.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a synthesis-ready secondary account of exactly the methodological schism this domain must characterize, disentangling the "origins" question (Political Marxism's specialty) from the "reproduction of an already-constituted system" question (world-systems theory's specialty), which is useful for showing how the two currents' disagreement is partly a difference in explanatory task.

  POSITION: Meta-theoretical reconstruction arguing the Brenner-Wallerstein dispute conflates distinct explanatory projects (origins vs. reproduction of capitalism).
  },
  keywords = {neo-smithian-marxism, brenner-critique, world-systems-theory, methodology, Medium}
}

@book{amin1976accumulation,
  author = {Amin, Samir},
  title = {Accumulation on a World Scale: A Critique of the Theory of Underdevelopment},
  year = {1976},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Amin develops a systematic theory of "unequal exchange" and "accumulation on a world scale," arguing that the capitalist world-economy is structurally polarized between "central" (autocentric) and "peripheral" (extraverted) formations, with value continuously transferred from periphery to center through trade conducted at prices that do not reflect equal labor values, producing and reproducing peripheral underdevelopment as a systemic effect of capital accumulation rather than a residual pre-capitalist condition.

  RELEVANCE: Alongside Wallerstein and Frank, this is a founding primary text of the dependency/world-systems current this domain must characterize, and its central category — unequal exchange as a mechanism of world-scale exploitation — is precisely the target of Brenner's and Weeks and Dore's production-centered Marxist critiques.

  POSITION: Foundational statement of unequal exchange and center-periphery polarization as the mechanism of capitalist accumulation on a world scale.
  },
  keywords = {unequal-exchange, samir-amin, accumulation, dependency-theory, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{amin2010lawworldwide,
  author = {Amin, Samir},
  title = {The Law of Worldwide Value},
  year = {2010},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Amin reformulates his theory of unequal exchange in terms of a "law of worldwide value," arguing that Marx's law of value must itself be reconceived at the scale of the capitalist world-economy, where the international mobility of capital but not of labor systematically generates and sustains a hierarchy of wage rates and profit rates between center and periphery that no purely national analysis of value can capture.

  RELEVANCE: Shows Amin explicitly attempting to relocate a core category of historical materialism — the labor theory of value — from the national to the world scale, making the theoretical ambition of this current (rather than only its empirical claims about underdevelopment) directly visible for comparison with orthodox and Political-Marxist value theory.

  POSITION: Reformulation of Marx's law of value as a "worldwide" law operating through structured wage and profit-rate hierarchies between core and periphery.
  },
  keywords = {unequal-exchange, samir-amin, law-of-value, dependency-theory, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{weeksdore1979internationalexchange,
  author = {Weeks, John and Dore, Elizabeth},
  title = {International Exchange and the Causes of Backwardness},
  journal = {Latin American Perspectives},
  year = {1979},
  volume = {6},
  number = {2},
  pages = {62--87},
  doi = {10.1177/0094582X7900600205},
  abstract = {The unequal development of the productive forces was shown by Marx to be a fundamental law of capitalist accumulation; and, in general, it is this law which produces uneven development on a world scale and inequality among countries. While all Marxists agree that the explanation of inequality in levels of development between countries lies in the laws of uneven development, there is less agreement upon the mechanism by which this uneven development is produced and reproduced on a world scale. One can distinguish two broad approaches, (a) those who explain inequality in levels of development among countries primarily in the sphere of circulation, emphasizing the appropriation of the surplus of one country by another country, and (b) those who place the cause of inequality in the sphere of production, and, thus, as a relationship between classes reproduced on a world scale. The purpose of this paper is to critique the former position and to develop further the latter.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Weeks and Dore argue, from within the Marxist tradition and independently of Brenner, that theories locating the cause of Third World "backwardness" in unequal exchange (circulation) rather than in class relations of production reproduced on a world scale are theoretically mistaken: exploitation is a relation between classes, not between countries, and international value transfers must be explained through this class relation rather than treated as a self-standing mechanism.

  RELEVANCE: Demonstrates that the "trade versus production" critique of dependency/unequal-exchange theory was not solely an external (Political-Marxist) attack but was also mounted from inside the Marxist development-studies literature contemporaneously with Brenner, showing the neo-Smithian-Marxism charge reflected a broader fault line within 1970s Marxism rather than a single author's idiosyncratic polemic.

  POSITION: Internal Marxist critique of circulationist unequal-exchange theory, insisting exploitation and uneven development must be theorized through class relations of production reproduced at world scale.
  },
  keywords = {unequal-exchange, dependency-theory, class-relations, marxist-critique, Medium}
}

@article{kvangraven2020beyondstereotype,
  author = {Kvangraven, Ingrid Harvold},
  title = {Beyond the Stereotype: Restating the Relevance of the Dependency Research Programme},
  journal = {Development and Change},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {52},
  number = {1},
  pages = {76--112},
  doi = {10.1111/dech.12593},
  abstract = {This article evaluates the relevance of dependency theory for understanding contemporary development challenges, especially in the light of changes in the global economy over the past 50 years. In order to do so, the article rectifies previous misunderstandings of the scholarship and offers a new definition of dependency theory as a research programme, rather than a singular theory. Four core tenets of this research programme are identified: a global historical approach; theorizing of the polarizing tendencies of global capitalism; a focus on structures of production; and a focus on the specific constraints faced by peripheral economies. While each of these elements can be found in many contemporary theories, what makes dependency theory unique — and a particularly strong research programme — is the combination of these elements. The article demonstrates how this approach provides a deep and broad understanding that is necessary to appreciate the persistence of uneven development with reference to two case studies, namely successful industrialization in South Korea, and how the fragmentation of global value chains has impacted industrialization in Indonesia.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kvangraven reconstructs dependency theory as a "research programme" with four core tenets — a global historical approach, theorization of capitalism's polarizing tendencies, an explicit focus on structures of production (not merely exchange), and attention to peripheral economies' specific structural constraints — and argues that critiques such as Brenner's, which characterize the school as reducing development to external, circulationist causes, rest on a caricature that ignores the production-focused strand of the tradition (including Frank's own case studies).

  RELEVANCE: Offers the most direct, contemporary rebuttal available to the neo-Smithian-Marxism charge central to this domain, arguing that the charge mischaracterizes at least part of the dependency tradition and proposing a reconstructed definition that foregrounds production relations at the global scale rather than mere trade/circulation.

  POSITION: Contemporary defense and reconstruction of dependency theory as a production-centered, not merely circulationist, research programme, against Brenner-style critiques.
  },
  keywords = {dependency-theory, neo-smithian-marxism, brenner-critique, research-programme, High}
}

@article{song2015reorienting,
  author = {Song, Hae-Yung},
  title = {Reorienting the Critique of the Capitalist World System beyond the Dichotomy between Trade vs. Production Relations},
  journal = {Journal of World-Systems Research},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {21},
  pages = {174--183},
  doi = {10.5195/jwsr.2015.533},
  abstract = {The question of 'capitalist transition' has been one of the central themes of Marxist scholarship including world-system analysis, and much effort has been made to identify how the capitalist mode of production came into being at a particular time in a particular location, and what historical conditions gave rise to it. The debate between Andre Gunder Frank and more broadly world-system analysis and Robert Brenner in the 1970s constitutes one of the key instances of this debate. Since Brenner called world-system analysis 'neo-Smithian' and criticized it for confusing the generic concept of market economy with capitalism (namely, capitalist social relations), the debate over capitalist transition has often been described as a contention over whether trade or production should be given primacy in explaining the emergence of capitalism.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Song argues that framing the Frank/Wallerstein–Brenner dispute as a binary choice between "trade" and "production" primacy obscures what is actually at stake: a disagreement over the proper boundary and scale of the unit of analysis — Brenner's nationally/regionally bounded social formation versus world-systems analysis's globally totalized world-economy — and proposes reorienting the critique toward this question of scale and totality rather than the trade/production dichotomy itself.

  RELEVANCE: Directly reframes the central methodological dispute this domain must present, offering a more precise diagnostic vocabulary (unit-of-analysis and scale, rather than trade-vs-production) for characterizing what divides world-systems theory from Political Marxism and orthodox historical materialism.

  POSITION: Meta-critical reframing of the Brenner-Frank/Wallerstein debate as fundamentally about scale and unit of analysis rather than trade versus production.
  },
  keywords = {neo-smithian-marxism, brenner-critique, world-systems-theory, unit-of-analysis, Medium}
}

@article{stephens2016accidentalmarxist,
  author = {Stephens, Cody},
  title = {The Accidental Marxist: Andre Gunder Frank and the "Neo-Marxist" Theory of Underdevelopment, 1958--1967},
  journal = {Modern Intellectual History},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {15},
  number = {2},
  pages = {411--442},
  doi = {10.1017/s1479244316000123},
  abstract = {Based on newly available archival records, this article examines the life and thought of Andre Gunder Frank from his years as a graduate student in development economics to the publication of his first and most influential book. A closer look at the evolution of Frank's thought provides new insight into the relationship of his brand of "neo-Marxist" development theories with both classical Marxism and modernization theory. Frank interpreted Marxist political debates according to the categories of thought of 1950s American development economics, and in doing so he both misinterpreted fundamental aspects of Marxism and simultaneously generated lively theoretical debates that remain relevant today.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stephens traces the intellectual formation of Frank's dependency theory in the decade before its canonical 1966/1967 statements, showing that Frank arrived at his "development of underdevelopment" thesis through an idiosyncratic synthesis of neoclassical economics, area-studies fieldwork in Latin America, and only belatedly an explicit engagement with Marxism — complicating any straightforward genealogy of dependency theory as a simple application of pre-existing Marxist categories to the periphery.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an intellectual-historical corrective useful for this domain's account of how dependency theory extended (or, on this account, only retrospectively aligned itself with) historical materialism's categories, cautioning against reading Frank's mature "neo-Marxist" self-presentation back into his formative theoretical development.

  POSITION: Intellectual history complicating the standard genealogy of Frank's dependency theory as a straightforward extension of orthodox Marxist categories.
  },
  keywords = {gunder-frank, dependency-theory, intellectual-history, Medium}
}

@article{kay2011andregunderfrank,
  author = {Kay, Cristóbal},
  title = {Andre Gunder Frank: 'Unity in Diversity' from the Development of Underdevelopment to the World System},
  journal = {New Political Economy},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {16},
  number = {4},
  pages = {523--538},
  doi = {10.1080/13563467.2011.597501},
  abstract = {The purpose of this article is to critically review the work of Andre Gunder Frank. This is no easy task given the prolific and controversial nature of his life work. His main distinction is as a paradigm breaker and a paradigm maker. Frank is one of the founders of contemporary world system theory. He coined some memorable expressions such as the 'development of underdevelopment' and 'Re-Orient'. Indeed, these two concepts highlight two distinct phases in his work. His first phase is characterised by his writings on dependency theory and his initial understanding of world system theory broadly in line with Amin, Arrighi and Wallerstein. His second phase is distinguished by what he considers to be the 'Eurocentric' interpretation of world system theory of Wallerstein and others as well as by his critique of his own earlier work. While some of Frank's analyses and assertions proved to be wrong, he provided much inspiration to a new generation of scholars and activists, some of whom provided the necessary empirical evidence and theoretical rigour lacking in parts of Frank's work.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kay periodizes Frank's intellectual trajectory into two phases — an initial dependency-theory phase closely aligned with Amin, Arrighi, and Wallerstein's world-systems analysis, and a later phase in which Frank repudiated even Wallerstein's version of world-systems analysis as insufficiently non-Eurocentric, culminating in his ReOrient thesis of a continuous Asia-centered world economy — while noting that several of Frank's specific empirical claims did not hold up, even as his big-picture questions proved generative for later scholars.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes that world-systems/dependency theory is not a single stable current but underwent its own internal critique of Eurocentrism from within (Frank against Wallerstein), which matters for this domain's account of how thoroughly the school actually escaped a Eurocentric frame versus merely relocating it.

  POSITION: Biographical-intellectual account of Frank's shift from dependency theory and early world-systems analysis to a self-consciously anti-Eurocentric "world system" of continuous Asian centrality.
  },
  keywords = {gunder-frank, dependency-theory, world-systems-theory, eurocentrism, Medium}
}

@article{petras1981dependencyworldsystem,
  author = {Petras, James},
  title = {Dependency and World System Theory},
  journal = {Latin American Perspectives},
  year = {1981},
  volume = {8},
  number = {3-4},
  pages = {148--155},
  doi = {10.1177/0094582x8100800310},
  abstract = {World system theory (so designated by one of its major practitioners, Immanuel Wallerstein, 1975) is derived from the intellectual heritage found in the critique of the developmentalist perspective of liberal political economy. This critique was articulated in the work of Paul Baran (1957), A. G. Frank (1967, 1969), Theotonio Dos Santos (1971), and other dependency theorists. Recently, dependency has achieved a new status in the attention given to the unequal exchange theses of Arghiri Emmanuel (1972), Samir Amin's discussion (1974) of the accumulation of world capital, and Immanuel Wallerstein's historical interpretation (1974, 1976) of the rise of a single capitalist world economy. The basic framework of a world systems theorist gives rise to the basic categories of analysis: core, semiperiphery and periphery, or metropole and satellite. The real innovation of the world systems approach lies in the choice of the primary unit of analysis: the capitalist world economy. All phenomena are to be explained in terms of their consequence for both the whole of the system and its parts.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Writing while the dependency and world-systems currents were still consolidating, Petras compares and contrasts the two approaches' treatment of class, state, and the international division of labor, arguing that both need firmer grounding in concrete class analysis of specific social formations if they are to avoid collapsing global structural description into a substitute for class-based political analysis.

  RELEVANCE: An early, internally Marxist stock-taking of the dependency/world-systems current roughly contemporaneous with Brenner's critique, useful for showing that concerns about the adequacy of "class" as theorized at the world scale were being raised from within the tradition itself, not only by its Political-Marxist critics.

  POSITION: Internal Marxist comparative assessment of dependency and world-system theory's respective treatments of class and the state.
  },
  keywords = {dependency-theory, world-systems-theory, class-analysis, Medium}
}

@article{feliz2021notesunequalexchange,
  author = {Féliz, Mariano},
  title = {Notes For a Discussion on Unequal Exchange and the Marxist Theory of Dependency},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {29},
  number = {4},
  pages = {114--152},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341897},
  abstract = {The debate on the decline of the terms of trade in dependent countries was never fully integrated into the Marxist theory of dependency. The attempt to articulate it through the category of unequal exchange was not particularly systematic. This paper seeks to recover those debates and will attempt to account for the relevant articulations in the light of a present revitalisation of studies in the field of Marxist dependency theory. To this end, we will recover the classical discussions around unequal exchange in order to discuss their points of contact with the Marxist theory of dependency and some contemporary debates around the transfer of value and the super-exploitation of labour and nature.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Féliz revisits the classical debates on unequal exchange (Emmanuel, Amin) and argues they were never systematically integrated into the "Marxist theory of dependency" associated with Marini and the Latin American dependencia marxista tradition, then reconstructs the connections between unequal exchange, the transfer of value, and the "super-exploitation" of labor and nature as a basis for a revitalized Marxist dependency theory.

  RELEVANCE: Written for the flagship journal of contemporary orthodox/open Marxism, this piece exemplifies the ongoing internal reconstruction of dependency theory in explicitly Marxist (value-theoretic) terms, directly engaging the categories (value transfer, exploitation, super-exploitation) that this domain's key questions ask about, and shows the current remains an active site of theoretical development rather than a closed historical episode.

  POSITION: Reconstruction of unequal exchange and value-transfer theory as integral, underdeveloped components of the Marxist theory of dependency.
  },
  keywords = {unequal-exchange, dependency-theory, marxist-theory-of-dependency, value-transfer, High}
}

@article{chian2025wallersteinafricanist,
  author = {Chian, Sam},
  title = {Immanuel Wallerstein as Africanist: from Modernisation to Marxism in the 1960s},
  journal = {Review of African Political Economy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {52},
  number = {183},
  doi = {10.62191/roape-2025-0001},
  abstract = {Immanuel Wallerstein's intellectual journey toward developing world-systems analysis began with his significant involvement in African studies during the 1960s. This article explores how Wallerstein's decade as an Africanist, marked by his deep engagement with the decolonisation movements across the continent, laid the foundation for his later Marxist-oriented critique of the capitalist world-economy. Through his work in Africa, Wallerstein progressively distanced himself from modernisation theory and embraced a Marxist perspective, largely informed by his interactions with figures like Frantz Fanon, Kwame Nkrumah and Amílcar Cabral. Initially focused on the nation state and independence movements in Africa, Wallerstein's scholarship increasingly recognised the limitations of methodological nationalism and the need to situate African political and economic conditions within the framework of historical capitalism.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chian shows that Wallerstein's world-systems Marxism was not an a priori theoretical commitment applied to Africa from outside, but emerged out of his decade of fieldwork on African decolonization and his engagement with Fanon, Nkrumah, and Cabral, which led him to reject methodological nationalism and modernization theory in favor of situating African political economy within the framework of historical capitalism as a whole.

  RELEVANCE: Directly supports this domain's account of world-systems theory as a genuinely non-Eurocentric extension of historical materialism by tracing its origin to anti-colonial African political thought and struggle rather than to a purely metropolitan theoretical innovation later applied to the periphery.

  POSITION: Intellectual history locating the genesis of Wallerstein's Marxist world-systems analysis in his engagement with African decolonization and anti-colonial thought.
  },
  keywords = {wallerstein, world-systems-theory, decolonization, africanist, intellectual-history, Medium}
}

@article{deoliveirakvangraven2023backtodakar,
  author = {de Oliveira, Felipe Antunes and Kvangraven, Ingrid Harvold},
  title = {Back to Dakar: Decolonizing International Political Economy through Dependency Theory},
  journal = {Review of International Political Economy},
  year = {2023},
  doi = {10.1080/09692290.2023.2169322},
  abstract = {Whereas the field of International Political Economy (IPE) included a diversity of voices at its outset, histories of the field tend to marginalize certain contributions - particularly those from the Global South. The endeavor to decolonize IPE offers an opportunity to look back at IPE's history, re-discover the marginalized voices, and imagine new possible futures. This article engages with contemporary calls to decolonize IPE and proposes an alternative route to do so by recovering dependency theory. We argue that dependency theory can be conceptualized as a peripheral IPE perspective that was committed to thinking from the Global South and to producing politically engaged scholarship just as the field was being formed. The article elaborates on the key tenets of dependency theory, contrasting it with mainstream IPE, and putting it in dialogue with decolonial approaches. To demonstrate the simultaneous non-Eurocentric, anti-colonial, and policy-oriented potential of dependency theory, we recover a foundational moment that disciplinary histories of IPE have forgotten: the 1972 Dakar conference, organized by Samir Amin, with the participation of leading Latin American and African dependency scholars.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: De Oliveira and Kvangraven recover the 1972 Dakar conference, organized by Amin and bringing together Latin American and African dependency scholars, as a suppressed founding moment of a genuinely Global South-centered international political economy, arguing that dependency theory's marginalization from mainstream IPE's disciplinary self-history was itself a form of epistemic Eurocentrism rather than a reflection of the theory's explanatory weakness.

  RELEVANCE: Substantiates this domain's claim that dependency/world-systems theory shows historical materialism's application "beyond a Eurocentric frame" with a concrete institutional and biographical episode (the Dakar conference), complementing Chian's account of Wallerstein and providing material for contrasting decolonial critiques of Eurocentrism with the separate trade-vs-production methodological critique from Political Marxism.

  POSITION: Decolonial historiographic recovery of dependency theory as a marginalized Global South current within international political economy.
  },
  keywords = {dependency-theory, samir-amin, decolonization, global-south, disciplinary-history, Medium}
}

@article{pradella2017marxglobalsouth,
  author = {Pradella, Lucia},
  title = {Marx and the Global South: Connecting History and Value Theory},
  journal = {Sociology},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {51},
  number = {1},
  doi = {10.1177/0038038516661267},
  abstract = {This article interrogates Marx's critique of political economy in the context of the global South and southern epistemologies. It first traces the contradictory roots of a non-Eurocentric conception of history within Adam Smith. Recovering Marx's silenced sociologies of colonialism in his writings and notebooks, it then shows that Marx incorporated colonialism and imperialism into his analysis of accumulation. The antagonism between wage-labour and capital needs to be understood as a global tendency, encompassing a hierarchy of forms of exploitation and oppression. Marx's support for the Taiping revolution (1850-1864) played a crucial, albeit often ignored, role in his theorisation. It allowed him to recognise the living potential for anti-colonial struggles and international solidarities, thus breaking with Eurocentric accounts of history.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Pradella argues, against readings of Marx as a Eurocentric stage theorist, that Marx's own later writings and notebooks incorporate colonialism and imperialism directly into his analysis of accumulation, theorizing the wage-labor/capital antagonism as a global tendency encompassing a hierarchy of exploitation forms across colonizer and colonized societies alike.

  RELEVANCE: Provides textual grounding, independent of Wallerstein/Frank/Amin's own theoretical innovations, for the claim that a world-scale, anti-Eurocentric extension of historical-materialist categories has warrant in Marx's own texts — relevant to assessing whether world-systems/dependency theory should be read as extending Marx's project or correcting a genuine Eurocentric limitation within it.

  POSITION: Textual-historical argument that Marx's own mature writings already incorporate a global, anti-Eurocentric analysis of accumulation, colonialism, and value.
  },
  keywords = {marx, eurocentrism, colonialism, value-theory, global-south, Medium}
}

@article{geneciran2024globalhistoricalmaterialism,
  author = {Geneciran, Joma},
  title = {Global Historical Materialism and Decoloniality},
  journal = {Review of African Political Economy},
  year = {2024},
  doi = {10.62191/roape-2024-0035},
  abstract = {This debate piece offers a global historical materialist critique of decolonial theory, engaging Walter Mignolo's work. It argues that decolonial literature, by creating abstract universals like modernity/coloniality, disarticulates theory from specific social formations and historical contexts. The author proposes eight methodological questions to demystify decolonial literature and reintegrate theory into history. Using Mignolo's writings, the paper demonstrates how decolonial thinking can lead to political detachment and ineffective transformative strategies. The critique emphasises grounding theory in concrete social formations to render history legible and serve national liberation movements. By contrasting decolonial abstractions with historical examples from anti-colonial struggles on and beyond the African continent, it highlights decolonial thought's limitations in addressing capitalism as a world system. It advocates for a global historical materialist analysis that integrates capitalism's interconnected nature and reintroduces the social formation as a unit of analysis for understanding social relations.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Geneciran defends a "global historical materialism" against decolonial theory (via a critique of Mignolo), arguing that decolonial abstractions like "modernity/coloniality" disarticulate theory from specific social formations and historical contexts, and that a properly world-scale historical materialism must reintroduce the social formation as a unit of analysis to render anti-colonial history legible and politically actionable.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a contemporary, explicitly self-identified "global historical materialist" position that both claims the world-systems/dependency legacy (world capitalism as an interconnected totality) and criticizes decolonial theory for abandoning the social-formation-level class analysis that this domain's methodological debates (Brenner vs. Wallerstein/Frank) turn on — showing the debate over scale and unit of analysis persists in a new, decolonial-theory register.

  POSITION: Defense of a social-formation-grounded "global historical materialism" against decolonial theory's abstraction from concrete class analysis.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, decoloniality, world-capitalism, social-formation, Medium}
}
